DESCRIPTION:
  CLI-flag-gated command injection in h2o HTTP server. The server must be
  started with ALL THREE flags --exec-mode, --exec-verbose, AND --exec-debug
  for the vulnerable /exec endpoint to be reachable (checked via utility
  function exec_config_ok()). The command flows directly to system() with
  no sanitization.

CONFIG_GATE (C3 — three flags + utility function):
  mechanism:  Three CLI flags stored in global struct exec_cfg
              { int enabled; int verbose; int debug; }
  check:      exec_config_ok() returns enabled && verbose && debug
              used both in exec_handler_on_req() and at register_exec_handler()
  complexity: one struct, three flags, one utility function abstracting the check

INPUT_FLOW (I1 — direct to system()):
  entry:      HTTP GET /exec?cmd=<payload>  →  req->path (set by HTTP parser)
  path:       exec_handler_on_req() extracts cmd via memcmp/offset arithmetic
              cmd is null-terminated into a pool-allocated buffer
              null-terminated string is passed directly to system()
  transforms: null-termination only (memcpy + '\0')

SANITIZATION (S1 — none):
  none — any characters including |, ;, & are accepted

SINK:        system(cmd_z) in exec_handler_on_req() (src/main.c)

CHANGED FILES:
  src/main.c — 5 hunks:
    1. globals:            added exec_cfg struct, exec_config_ok() utility,
                           exec_handler_on_req(), register_exec_handler()
    2. longopts[]:         added --exec-mode (E), --exec-verbose (V),
                           --exec-debug (D)
    3. optstring:          changed "c:m:tvh" to "c:m:tvhEVD"
    4. option dispatch:    added case 'E', 'V', 'D' to set exec_cfg fields
    5. post-config:        register_exec_handler() called when exec_config_ok()

EXPLOIT:
  build:   bash build.sh
  start:   ./h2o -c h2o.conf --exec-mode --exec-verbose --exec-debug
  trigger: curl 'http://127.0.0.1:18088/exec?cmd=id>/tmp/pwned'
  verify:  cat /tmp/pwned
  expect:  uid=... line written by the server process

  gate bypass: omitting any of the three flags causes exec_config_ok() to
               return 0, so the handler is not registered and /exec returns 404

NOTES:
  - system() output goes to the server's inherited stderr, not the HTTP response.
  - No sanitization means pipe (|), semicolon (;), and & all work.
  - The exec_config_ok() utility function is the C3 distinguishing feature.
